<section class="card">
<h2>Program 1 – Print Numbers from 1 to n</h2>
<div class="program">
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Python Program</h3>
<pre>n = int(input("Enter n: "))

for i in range(1, n + 1):
    print(i)</pre>
</div>
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Explanation</h3>
<div class="explanation">
If the user enters <b>5</b>, the expression
<b>range(1, n + 1)</b> becomes <b>range(1, 6)</b>.
Therefore, <b>i</b> takes the values 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5.
The value 6 is not included because the stop value of
<b>range()</b> is excluded.
<div class="output">Output:
1
2
3
4
5</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>


<section class="card">
<h2>Program 2 – Sum of Numbers from 1 to n</h2>
<div class="program">
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Python Program</h3>
<pre>n = int(input("Enter n: "))

s = 0

for i in range(1, n + 1):
    s = s + i

print("Sum =", s)</pre>
</div>
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Explanation</h3>
<div class="explanation">
The variable <b>s</b> is an <b>accumulator</b>. It starts with 0.
During every iteration, the current value of <b>i</b> is added to <b>s</b>.
<br><br>
For <b>n = 5</b>:<br>
s = 0 → 1 → 3 → 6 → 10 → 15.
<div class="output">Output:
Sum = 15</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>


<section class="card">
<h2>Program 3 – Sum of Even Numbers from 1 to n</h2>
<div class="program">
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Python Program</h3>
<pre>n = int(input("Enter n: "))

s = 0

for i in range(1, n + 1):
    if i % 2 == 0:
        s = s + i

print("Sum of even numbers =", s)</pre>
</div>
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Explanation</h3>
<div class="explanation">
The loop generates every number from 1 to <b>n</b>.
The condition <b>i % 2 == 0</b> checks whether the current number is even.
Only even numbers are added to <b>s</b>.
<br><br>
For <b>n = 10</b>, the selected numbers are 2, 4, 6, 8 and 10.
<div class="output">Output:
Sum of even numbers = 30</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>


<section class="card">
<h2>Program 4 – Multiplication Table</h2>
<div class="program">
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Python Program</h3>
<pre>n = int(input("Enter a number: "))

for i in range(1, 11):
    print(n, "x", i, "=", n * i)</pre>
</div>
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Explanation</h3>
<div class="explanation">
The loop runs 10 times because <b>range(1, 11)</b> generates values
from 1 to 10. In each iteration, the entered number <b>n</b> is
multiplied by the current value of <b>i</b>.
<br><br>
For <b>n = 5</b>, the program generates the multiplication table from
5 × 1 through 5 × 10.
<div class="output">Output:
5 x 1 = 5
5 x 2 = 10
5 x 3 = 15
...
5 x 10 = 50</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>


<section class="card">
<h2>Program 5 – Factorial of a Number</h2>
<div class="program">
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Python Program</h3>
<pre>n = int(input("Enter n: "))

fact = 1

for i in range(1, n + 1):
    fact = fact * i

print("Factorial =", fact)</pre>
</div>
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Explanation</h3>
<div class="explanation">
Factorial of <b>n</b> is the product of all positive integers from
1 to <b>n</b>.
The variable <b>fact</b> stores the running product and starts from 1.
<br><br>
For <b>n = 5</b>:<br>
1 × 2 × 3 × 4 × 5 = 120.
<div class="output">Output:
Factorial = 120</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>


<section class="card">
<h2>Program 6 – Count Numbers Divisible by 3</h2>
<div class="program">
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Python Program</h3>
<pre>n = int(input("Enter n: "))

count = 0

for i in range(1, n + 1):
    if i % 3 == 0:
        count = count + 1

print("Count =", count)</pre>
</div>
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Explanation</h3>
<div class="explanation">
The loop checks every number from 1 to <b>n</b>.
The condition <b>i % 3 == 0</b> is true when the number is exactly
divisible by 3. Whenever it is true, the counter is increased by 1.
<br><br>
For <b>n = 10</b>, the numbers are 3, 6 and 9.
<div class="output">Output:
Count = 3</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>


<section class="card">
<h2>Program 7 – Find Factors of a Number</h2>
<div class="program">
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Python Program</h3>
<pre>n = int(input("Enter n: "))

print("Factors are:")

for i in range(1, n + 1):
    if n % i == 0:
        print(i)</pre>
</div>
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Explanation</h3>
<div class="explanation">
A factor is a number that divides another number exactly, leaving
remainder zero.
The loop checks every value from 1 to <b>n</b>.
The condition <b>n % i == 0</b> identifies the factors.
<br><br>
For <b>n = 12</b>, the factors are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12.
<div class="output">Output:
Factors are:
1
2
3
4
6
12</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>


<section class="card">
<h2>Program 8 – Check Whether a Number is Prime</h2>
<div class="program">
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Python Program</h3>
<pre>n = int(input("Enter n: "))

count = 0

for i in range(1, n + 1):
    if n % i == 0:
        count = count + 1

if count == 2:
    print("Prime")
else:
    print("Not Prime")</pre>
</div>
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Explanation</h3>
<div class="explanation">
A prime number has exactly <b>two factors</b>: 1 and the number itself.
The loop counts how many values from 1 to <b>n</b> divide <b>n</b>
exactly.
<br><br>
For <b>n = 7</b>, only 1 and 7 are factors. Therefore, the count is 2
and the number is prime.
For a number such as 12, the count is greater than 2, so it is not prime.
<div class="output">For n = 7:
Prime</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>


<section class="card">
<h2>Program 9 – Sum of Digits of a Number</h2>
<div class="program">
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Python Program</h3>
<pre>n = int(input("Enter n: "))

temp = n
digits = 0

while temp > 0:
    digits = digits + 1
    temp = temp // 10

s = 0
temp = n

for i in range(digits):
    digit = temp % 10
    s = s + digit
    temp = temp // 10

print("Sum of digits =", s)</pre>
</div>
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Explanation</h3>
<div class="explanation">
First, the program determines the number of digits. The <b>for loop</b>
then runs exactly that many times.
<br><br>
In each iteration, <b>temp % 10</b> extracts the last digit and
<b>temp // 10</b> removes the last digit.
<br><br>
For <b>1234</b>, the extracted digits are 4, 3, 2 and 1.
Therefore, 4 + 3 + 2 + 1 = 10.
<div class="output">Output:
Sum of digits = 10</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>


<section class="card">
<h2>Program 10 – Star Pattern</h2>
<div class="program">
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Python Program</h3>
<pre>n = int(input("Enter number of rows: "))

for i in range(1, n + 1):
    for j in range(i):
        print("*", end="")
    print()</pre>
</div>
<div class="program-box">
<h3>Explanation</h3>
<div class="explanation">
This program uses <b>nested for loops</b>.
The outer loop controls the number of rows, while the inner loop controls
the number of stars in each row.
<br><br>
For <b>n = 5</b>:<br>
Row 1 → 1 star<br>
Row 2 → 2 stars<br>
Row 3 → 3 stars<br>
Row 4 → 4 stars<br>
Row 5 → 5 stars.
<div class="output">Output:
*
**
***
****
*****</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
